p='david_arnold';s='tq234_arnold_science2000'
P(p,
'A historian of South Asia whose research connects colonial power with medicine, science and environment. The introduction to Science, Technology and Medicine in Colonial India rejects a single trajectory from European discovery to passive reception overseas. It treats scientific knowledge as socially situated, explicitly drawing on Shapin, while insisting that a history of science in India must also be a history of India. Arnold criticizes Basalla’s diffusion model for flattening differences between colonial situations, marginalizing local agency and assuming a beneficial developmental sequence. Yet he also rejects a continuity story that understates colonial disruption. His earlier Colonizing the Body acknowledges a debt to Foucault while distinguishing its focus on the colonial state. This qualified use of theory accompanies his participation in the founding Subaltern Studies collective; neither connection licenses reading every later project as the application of a single school.',
'New primary publisher sample selected introduction pp. 1–2 and 9–12 read for methodological argument; intervening historical material and remainder not claimed as a complete reading. Earlier indexed Colonizing the Body p. 7 remains a narrow witness. Basalla and Shapin originals unread. Scholarly models only: no traditional knowledge or community narrative content retained. Warwick’s older profile establishes professional history, not current life status.',
sources=[S(s,'David Arnold, Science, Technology and Medicine in Colonial India (Cambridge, 2000), selected introduction.','https://assets.cambridge.org/97805215/63192/sample/9780521563192wsc00.pdf','Publisher sample: selected pp. 1–2,9–12 and nn1–4,21–23 read. Situated science, colonial discontinuity and qualified criticism of Basalla. Not a full chapter/book or cited-original reading; no traditional knowledge content retained.')],
edges=[E(p,'shapin','steven_shapin',p,'Arnold uses Shapin’s account of the cultural situation of knowledge to frame colonial science.',s,'Primary introduction p. 1/n3, explicit quotation and application to the chapter’s social account. A specified conceptual resource, not personal mentorship.'),E(p,'science',p,'science','Challenges a uniform diffusionist account of colonial science through social context, local agency and differences between colonial regimes.',s,'Primary pp. 1–2,9–12, especially the criticism and partial retention of Basalla’s model. Not an assertion that science was simply an instrument of state power.')],
reads=[dict(source_id=s,locator='Selected introduction pp. 1–2,9–12/nn1–4,21–23')],holds=['Basalla has no individual endpoint; criticism remains in biography and field relationship. Complete selected books and recent article unread. No new current-life claim from publication metadata.'])

p='j_r_mcneill';s='pe131_extras_mcneill_2003';a='p4c_mcneill_aha_2020'
P(p,
'An environmental historian whose work connects ecological change with global and regional histories. His field survey credits the Annales with an inspiring general approach while distinguishing that resource from the relatively limited environmental history its early practitioners actually wrote. He identifies Crosby’s exchanges-and-ecology studies as landmarks without presenting a unanimous reception or making pathogens a substitute for human responsibility. His 2020 presidential address extends the methodological question: scientific evidence can illuminate histories poorly documented in writing, but the availability of a tool must not dictate an explanation. He warns against essentializing genetic identities and monocausal climate histories, and recognizes unequal resources and authority within interdisciplinary collaborations. Something New Under the Sun and Mosquito Empires supply different scales for this programme; their publisher descriptions do not replace reading their complete arguments.',
'Existing passage reading of 2003 survey retained: pp. 14–16,24,32–33,37–39,42, with the original research notes’ limits. Fresh selected professional-method passages of 2020 address read: opening peak-document question, implications for environmental history, and concluding hazards/collaboration sections, nn36,39–44. Scientific studies and full monographs unread. Reported criticism by other readers of Crosby is not reassigned to McNeill. John R. McNeill is not William McNeill.',
existing=[s,a],
edges=[E(p,'annales','annales',p,'McNeill credits the Annales’ general historical approach while qualifying its actual contribution to environmental research.',s,'Previously read survey p. 14, distinguishing Braudel’s relatively fixed environment from Le Roy Ladurie’s environmental books. Intellectual resource with limits, not formal school membership.'),E(p,'crosby','alfred_crosby',p,'McNeill identifies Crosby’s exchanges-and-ecology studies as landmarks in the development of global environmental history.',s,'Previously read pp. 24,32–33,42. Named historiographical appraisal, not a claim of personal training. The p. 33 criticism is attributed to some readers, not adopted as McNeill’s own verdict.')],
replacements=[R(p,1,p,'environment','Advocates combining textual and scientific evidence while warning against monocausal explanations and tool-driven historical questions.',a,'Fresh selected 2020 address: environmental-history discussion and final hazards/collaboration sections, nn36,39–44. Methods and self-qualification, not certification of scientific findings.')],
reads=[dict(source_id=s,locator='Previously checked pp. 14–16,24,32–33,37–39,42',fresh=False),dict(source_id=a,locator='Fresh selected opening, environmental implications, hazards and collaboration sections; nn36,39–44')],holds=['Complete monographs and cited science unread. No John/William McNeill conflation or imported influence from a namesake citation.'])

p='patrick_geary';s='tq234_geary_fanning2002'
P(p,
'A medieval historian of social relations, objects and the production of a remembered past. The opening of “Sacred Commodities” asks what makes relics exchangeable within a particular medieval setting, rather than assuming a timeless market for all objects. His work on remembrance, as described by the IAS, treats preservation and destruction of records as connected historical processes. The Myth of Nations brings research on changing early medieval identities into a criticism of modern nationalist claims to ancient continuity. Steven Fanning’s substantive review explains Geary’s use of ethnogenesis scholarship and credits the book’s central argument while warning that its general survey contains questionable assertions and overly broad generalizations. That reception distinguishes a useful intervention for nonspecialists from an unquestionable reference work. The argument concerns changing identifications and political uses of history, not the absence of all meaningful medieval identities.',
'Fanning’s 2002 H-France review selected opening historiographical paragraphs and concluding appraisal read; the intervening account of communities and the coda are excluded from retained evidence. The underlying Myth of Nations and cited ethnogenesis originals unread. Earlier primary chapter opening and IAS institutional account keep their narrower scopes. No oral narrative, traditional knowledge, community genealogy or coda details retained.',
sources=[S(s,'Steven Fanning, review of Patrick Geary, The Myth of Nations, H-France Review 2, no. 124 (November 2002).','https://www.h-france.net/vol2reviews/fanning.html','Selected opening account of ethnogenesis scholarship and final evaluation read. Credits central argument while criticizing broad generalization and reliability as a factual reference. Underlying book unread; community/coda material excluded.')],
replacements=[R(p,3,p,'nationalismstudies','Challenges claims of unbroken ancient national identity by tracing changing medieval identifications and the modern political uses of their history.',['tc234_geary_ias',s],'IAS account and Fanning’s selected opening/conclusion. Named-reader mediation retained; not denial of all medieval collective identities.')],
reads=[dict(source_id=s,locator='Opening historiographical paragraphs; concluding general-reader appraisal and cautions')],holds=['Fanning, Wenskus and Wolfram have no existing individual endpoints; no invented influence arrow. Full medieval books unread; blocked later memory retrospective remains unread.'])

p='mike_davis';s='tc234_davis_marxist';f='tc234_davis_famine'
P(p,
'A historian of cities, capitalism and environmental disaster. His account of learning Marxism credits Brenner’s Capital seminar with giving him confidence to connect political economy, labor history and urban ecology. He values historical and political questions over theory detached from them, and later returns to Marx while identifying limits in his treatment of cities, geography and the workers’ movement’s patriarchal assumptions. These are Davis’s critical assessments, not independently verified descriptions of every Marx text. In the publisher excerpt from Late Victorian Holocausts, he connects climatic instability to imperial systems that made populations vulnerable to famine. His explicit use of Raymond Williams helps frame nature as historically entangled with human activity. City of Quartz and Ecology of Fear mark related urban projects. Climate and political economy operate together here rather than turning weather into an autonomous explanation of catastrophe.',
'Freshly extended selected professional-intellectual passages of the authorized Marxism excerpt: seminar/theory paragraphs and later discussion of Marx’s blind spots. Personal and family material excluded. Earlier famine excerpt scope retained: opening argument and final methodological paragraphs, including Williams. Full books, Marx/Williams originals and numerical disaster estimates unread; the sweeping city claim is kept as Davis’s attributed criticism, not endorsed literally.',
existing=[s,f],
edges=[E(p,'marxresource','karl',p,'Davis’s account of Marxist study links Capital, crisis theory and historical debates to his research on labor, political economy and urban ecology.',s,'Primary excerpt paragraphs beginning That same year and Later the seminar, followed by his criticism of detached theory. Brenner’s seminar mediates the resource; no invented personal contact with Marx.'),E(p,'marxcrit',p,'karl','Davis identifies limits in Marx’s attention to cities, geography and patriarchal assumptions within the workers’ movement.',s,'Selected later paragraphs beginning The panoramic view and The patriarchal character. Attributed assessment, not adoption of every sweeping or numerical claim.',kind='critique'),E(p,'williams','raymond_williams',p,'Davis uses Williams’s account of nature and human history to frame the joint study of climatic and social processes.',f,'Earlier checked publisher excerpt’s concluding methodological paragraphs, explicit named quotation. Williams’s original text not independently read.')],
reads=[dict(source_id=s,locator='Extended selected seminar/theory and later critical-method paragraphs'),dict(source_id=f,locator='Previously read opening and concluding methodological paragraphs',fresh=False)],holds=['Brenner has no individual endpoint; named contribution stays in prose and evidence note. Do not turn criticism of theoretical detachment into wholesale rejection of Marxism.'])

p='hugh_trevor_roper';s='tq234_trevor_worden_extended2007'
P(p,
'A historian of early modern political, religious and intellectual conflicts who sought to combine narrative with social analysis. His later preface questions a simple Calvinism-to-capitalism explanation associated with Weber and Tawney and calls for European comparison. Blair Worden’s reading of the unfinished civil-war project explains Trevor-Roper’s resistance to deterministic accounts of revolution and his attention to decisions, personality and contingency. Worden also identifies limitations: a court-and-country thesis too narrow for the constitutional and religious demands of 1640–42, and an insufficient range of evidence and sympathy. His account records Elliott’s criticism of a draft and Trevor-Roper’s efforts to bring Continental historiography into Oxford. The failed authentication of the Hitler diaries in 1983 remains a serious later failure of source judgment; it is not made the cause of projects abandoned earlier.',
'Fresh extended selected reading of Worden’s 2007 memoir pp. 263–267 and 281–282, alongside previously checked p. 272. Primary later preface remains PDF pp. 6–7 only; essays and unpublished drafts are mediated through Worden. No original Elliott correspondence or civil-war manuscript read. Narrative/analysis comparison is Worden’s interpretation; the Scotland manuscript chronology remains separate from the later diary scandal.',
sources=[S(s,'Blair Worden, “Hugh Trevor-Roper, 1914–2003,” Proceedings of the British Academy 150 (2007): extended selected passages.','https://www.thebritishacademy.ac.uk/documents/1776/150p247.pdf','Selected pp. 263–267 and 281–282 read: unfinished civil-war project, contingency, Elliott’s draft criticism, limits of court/country and Continental research initiatives. Earlier p. 272 reading retained separately. Not the entire memoir or manuscripts.')],
edges=[E(p,'elliott','john_h_elliott',p,'Worden records Elliott’s criticism of Trevor-Roper’s civil-war manuscript as a contribution to its reconsideration.',s,'Named memoir p. 265, explicit February 1961 draft and need for revision. Original manuscript and correspondence unread; no particular Elliott criticism invented.'),E(p,'hill',p,'christopher_hill','Worden presents Trevor-Roper’s emphasis on contingency as resisting the deterministic tendency he saw in Hill’s and others’ accounts of revolution.',s,'Memoir pp. 265–266, comparing narrative with analytical traditions and explaining Trevor-Roper’s objection. Mediated criticism of a specified tendency, not a verdict on all Hill scholarship.',kind='critique'),E(p,'annales','annales',p,'Continental historiography informed Trevor-Roper’s attempts to widen historical research at Oxford.',s,'Memoir pp. 281–282 names Pirenne, Febvre and Braudel and describes planned research institute and Spooner appointment. Bounded methodological/institutional resource, not formal school affiliation.')],
reads=[dict(source_id=s,locator='Selected pp. 263–267,281–282'),dict(source_id='tc234_trevor_worden',locator='Previously checked p. 272',fresh=False)],holds=['Full essays, diaries and unpublished manuscripts unread. Worden’s criticism is retained in biography despite no individual Worden endpoint.'])
